course: arts
lesson: 15
title: Famous paintings and the stories behind them
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Famous paintings are famous for more than looks: a theft, a war, a mystery or a brave choice of style can make a picture known all over the world.
explain: Picture Chen at the Louvre in Paris, squeezed in a crowd of phones, staring at the Mona Lisa: it is small, about 77 by 53 centimetres, and part of its fame comes from being stolen in 1911 and found two years later.
explain: Artists choose their style on purpose: Van Gogh painted swirling skies to show feeling and movement, Munch showed fear, Picasso painted the horror of a bombed town, and old Flemish painters hid little stories in busy village scenes.
explain: Many paintings mix fact and legend, and some mysteries were later filled in by novels and films; it is wise to ask why an artist chose a style or a subject before mocking it, and to know which parts of a story are made up.
explain: People react in their own slang: in Britain "manky" means dirty or gross and "proper cracking up" means laughing a lot; "lit" means exciting, and "grim" means horrible.
fact: The Mona Lisa by Leonardo da Vinci is about 77 by 53 centimetres; it was stolen from the Louvre in 1911 and found two years later, which made headlines everywhere.
fact: Vincent van Gogh painted The Starry Night in 1889 at a hospital in France where he was being treated for mental illness, using swirling brushstrokes on purpose and writing about his ideas in letters to his brother.
fact: Edvard Munch, from Norway, made several versions of The Scream from 1893; he said the figure hears a scream passing through nature and covers its ears.
fact: The Great Wave off Kanagawa is a woodblock print by the Japanese artist Hokusai, made around 1831, showing a huge wave over small boats with Mount Fuji in the distance.
fact: Most experts say the Great Wave shows a huge storm wave out at sea, not a tsunami; because it was printed, many cheap copies were sold.
fact: Van Gogh painted a series of sunflower paintings in 1888 and 1889, with flowers at every stage from fresh to wilting, to decorate a room for his friend the painter Paul Gauguin.
fact: Kateryna Bilokur was a self-taught Ukrainian folk painter of incredibly detailed flowers; it is reported that Picasso admired her work when he saw it in Paris in 1954.
fact: Pablo Picasso painted Guernica in 1937 after the Spanish town of Guernica was bombed in the Spanish Civil War; it is almost 8 metres wide and became a famous protest against war.
fact: Pieter Bruegel the Elder, a Flemish painter of the 1500s, painted busy village scenes full of small stories, like a picture puzzle.
fact: Johannes Vermeer painted Girl with a Pearl Earring around 1665 in the Netherlands; who the girl was is unknown, and a later novel and film invented a story about her.
fact: Ilya Repin, born in Chuhuiv in Ukraine, finished Reply of the Zaporozhian Cossacks in 1891; it shows a legend of Cossacks writing a cheeky, insulting letter to the Ottoman sultan.
fact: Paintings of history often mix fact, legend and the painter's imagination.
exercise: Chen finds the Mona Lisa tiny. What is true? | It is about 77 by 53 cm, and fame comes from history too | It is the biggest painting in the world, filling a whole wall | It was painted last century for the Louvre | It is only famous because it is huge, so Chen must be far away
exercise: What happened to the Mona Lisa in 1911? | It was stolen and found two years later | It was painted for the very first time that year | It was cut into two separate pieces by a thief | It was sold to a big museum in Japan for millions
exercise: Liam's cousin asks if Van Gogh was "high" to paint swirls. What is true? | He chose that style on purpose to show feeling and movement | The swirls were a mistake he couldn't fix, so he kept them | He copied the swirls from a photograph | The sky really looked swirly that night, so he painted it exactly as it was
exercise: Where did Van Gogh paint The Starry Night? | At a hospital in France in 1889 | In a New York studio in 1950 | On a ship sailing to Japan in 1900 | In the Louvre in Paris
exercise: Ben calls The Scream "the emoji guy". What did Munch say the figure is doing? | Hearing a scream in nature and covering its ears | Screaming at a friend who stole his hat on the bridge | Singing a song very loudly | Yawning very widely after a long and tiring day
exercise: Aoife asks if The Great Wave is a tsunami. What do most experts say? | It is a huge storm wave out at sea | It is a giant tsunami hitting a city | It is a calm lake at night | It is a river in flood after heavy rain
exercise: Who made The Great Wave? | Hokusai, a Japanese artist | Picasso, a famous Spanish artist | Bilokur, a Ukrainian artist | Munch, a famous Norwegian artist
exercise: Why did The Great Wave reach so many people? | It was a print, so many cheap copies were sold | Only one copy exists, and it was kept secret | It was shown on television in 1831 | It was painted on the wall of a famous building in Tokyo
exercise: Ollie thinks Van Gogh's wilting sunflowers are "manky". Why paint them? | He liked showing life changing, fresh to wilting | He could only find dead flowers in the shops | He did not know how to paint fresh ones | Wilting flowers were the only ones painters were allowed to paint
exercise: Who were the sunflowers meant to decorate a room for? | His friend the painter Paul Gauguin | The king of France | A flower shop in Amsterdam | His old art teacher from school
exercise: Jack thinks Bilokur's flowers are a photo. What is true about her? | She was a self-taught Ukrainian folk painter | She went to the best art school in Paris | She took photographs and painted over them | She was a Japanese printmaker
exercise: Zara asks why anyone would paint Guernica. Why did Picasso? | As a protest after a town was bombed in a war | To decorate a happy children's room in his house | Because he was told to paint horses | To show a calm, sunny day in a quiet Spanish village
exercise: Ethan searches for the man fallen on the ice. Why are Bruegel's paintings like this? | They are busy scenes full of small stories | They were painted to be cut up for a puzzle game | They are boring scenes of empty, quiet fields | They were painted by many different people at once
exercise: Tiana hears nobody knows who the pearl earring girl was. What is true? | Her identity is unknown; the film's story is fiction | The film tells the exact true history of the girl | She was Vermeer's teacher | She was a famous queen of the Netherlands
exercise: Viktor sees Repin's Cossacks cracking up. What does the painting show? | A legend of Cossacks writing a cheeky letter to a sultan | An exact photo of a real meeting, taken in 1891 | Cossacks reading a school book | A wedding party in a palace, with music and dancing all night
exercise: Where was Repin born? | Chuhuiv in Ukraine | Paris, the capital of France | Oslo, the capital of Norway | Kyoto, an old city in Japan
test: Your mate goes, "famous paintings are always massive, that's why they're famous." What is wrong? | Many are small; fame comes from history and stories too | Nothing, every famous painting is huge | Famous paintings are always the smallest ones | Size is the only thing that matters
test: Liam's cousin smirks that Van Gogh "must've been high" for the swirls. What would Van Gogh's letters show? | He chose the style on purpose to show feeling | He painted while fast asleep in the night | He never wrote a letter | He hated the colour blue and only used it by accident
test: A cartoon shows The Scream figure yelling at the viewer. What does the cartoon get wrong? | Munch said the figure hears a scream and covers its ears | Nothing, the figure is clearly shouting at us | The figure is singing | The figure is laughing at a joke
test: Aoife's friend says, "It's just a wave, innit." What is closer to what experts say? | A huge storm wave at sea, with Mount Fuji behind | A tiny ripple in a pond | A tsunami hitting a city full of people | A wave in a swimming pool with a mountain painted behind
test: Ollie says sunflowers should only be painted fresh. What would have happened if Van Gogh had agreed? | We would lose paintings showing life changing | Nothing at all would change | All flowers in the world would stop wilting | He would have painted many more Starry Nights instead
test: Jack asks if Bilokur's flowers are a photo. What does this show? | Self-taught painters can reach amazing detail | Only photos can show detail | Bilokur used a camera | Folk painters cannot paint detail without going to a proper art school
test: Zara says Guernica is "grim". What is true about art and grim things? | Some great art shows terrible things so people remember | Art must always be pretty to be good | Grim pictures are never put in museums or shown anywhere | Picasso only painted happy pictures
test: Tiana says the pearl earring film is "what really happened". What is wise? | Enjoy it, but know the story is made up | Believe it, films about art are always true | The girl's real name is printed on the painting | The film was made by Vermeer himself, long ago
test: Viktor says Repin's painting shows exactly what happened. What is fairer? | It shows a legend and the painter's imagination | It is a photograph taken in 1891 | It shows only made-up animals | It is a copy of a modern film about Cossacks and sultans
test: What would happen if Ethan spent only two seconds on a Bruegel? | He would miss most of its little stories | He would see everything at once | The painting would change | He would find the fallen man straight away, without looking
test: Which painting is almost 8 metres wide? | Guernica | The Mona Lisa | Girl with a Pearl Earring | The Starry Night
exam: Identify the principal circumstance, other than its artistic merit, which contributed to the celebrity of the Mona Lisa. | Its theft from the Louvre in 1911 and recovery two years thereafter | Its exceptional dimensions exceeding those of all other paintings | Its completion in the twentieth century | Its exhibition upon national television in the course of the nineteenth century
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the composition of Guernica. | It was executed by Picasso in 1937 following the bombing of a Spanish town | It was executed by Van Gogh in 1889 in a French hospital | It depicts a peaceful festival in a Spanish village | It was commissioned as a decoration for a nursery in a Spanish palace by the king
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the identity of the subject of Girl with a Pearl Earring? | Her identity is unknown, and later narratives concerning her are fictional | She is established to have been a queen of the Netherlands | She is established to have been the instructor of Vermeer | Her name is inscribed upon the canvas
exam: Ascertain the reason for which the work of Hokusai entitled The Great Wave reached a numerous public. | It was a woodblock print reproduced in many inexpensive copies | It was displayed exclusively in a single private collection | It was painted upon a celebrated public building | It was broadcast by television in the nineteenth century
